.TH CUEILLE 1 "2026-07-31" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
cueille \- report where one work is readable without payment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" bashify: norun
.B cueille doi
\fIdoi\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B cueille author
\fIname\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B cueille title
\fIwords\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B cueille item
\fIidentifier\fR
.br
.B cueille
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cueille
is the gathering verb of
.BR bibliothecaire .
It asks the open finding aids one question about one work \- is this reachable
at all, and from where \- and prints their answers. It fetches no full text, it
opens no paywall, and it forms no opinion about the work.
.PP
The four probes are subcommands rather than flags, because each names a
different question and the flag namespace belongs to the shared runtime:
.TP
.B doi
Probe OpenAlex for the open\-access status of a DOI, then Unpaywall for a
second opinion. The two disagree often enough that the second fetch earns its
delay, so both run and both are printed.
.TP
.B author
Probe HAL for that author's deposits that carry full text. The filter is part
of the question: an unfiltered HAL search returns metadata\-only records, which
look like hits from a distance.
.TP
.B title
Probe HAL the same way, matching title words instead of an author.
.TP
.B item
Probe the archive.org metadata for one identifier: lending\-restricted or
downloadable, plus the size of the extracted text, because a ten\-kilobyte text
file on a three\-hundred\-page book is front matter wearing a book's name.
.PP
A negative result is a result.
.B cueille
distinguishes three outcomes that a careless probe collapses into one: the
work is reachable, the work is absent from an index that answered, and the
index did not answer. Only the first two are findings. The third exits
.B 6
and is never printed as an absence, because a dead route recorded as a checked
fact is the error this utility exists to stop repeating.
.PP
Politeness is a property of the tool, not of the caller. Requests are
sequential, separated by a fixed delay, carry a contact address, and stop at a
per\-work fetch ceiling. A bot wall is reported and obeyed; it is never worked
around.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of flags; everything after is a positional argument.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
.B cueille does not spend money.
It carries no \fB\-\-summon\fR
flag, and no subcommand of it can reach anything that bills. Every source it
reads is open to anyone: an index, a repository, a metadata endpoint. Asking
.B \-\-help
whether this can cost anything gets a plain no.
.PP
It does spend something, and the page names it so the boundary is not
mistaken for free. Each invocation spends fetches against public services that
owe it nothing: at most twenty per work, one at a time, with a delay before
each. The ceiling is per work rather than per session, so one unreachable
subject cannot consume a night's budget. The count spent is printed at the end
of every run, found or not found.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every probe completed. The work was located, or it was absent from indexes
that answered; both are answers.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. No subcommand, an unknown subcommand, or a
subcommand with no subject is one of these.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran with valid arguments and could not finish \- the fetch ceiling
was reached with probes still outstanding, so the report is partial.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: a probe did not complete. A refusal, a timeout, or a malformed reply
means this utility cannot see, which is a different fact from the work not
being there. The failing probe is named on standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/find\-open\-copy.py
The probe this verb fronts.
.TP
.I SOURCES.md
Where the outcome of a run belongs, dated, negative or not. Recording the
negative is what stops the next pass re\-spending these fetches.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the utility name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cueille --version
cueille (bashified)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask for a probe without naming a subject. The subcommand exists, the question
does not, so it is a usage error rather than an empty report:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cueille
cueille: name one of: doi, author, title, item
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR whois (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR ping (1),
.BR test (1),
.BR getopt (1)
.PP
Lineage:
.BR whois (1)
in querying public registries and printing what they say without
interpretation;
.BR ping (1)
in separating "no answer" from "answered no", which is the whole distinction
this utility keeps;
.BR test (1)
in reserving 0 for success and giving distinct nonzero codes distinct
meanings;
.BR getopt (1)
in the flag grammar, including
.B \-\-
as end\-of\-flags.
.SH HISTORY
Written 2026\-07\-31, before
.B cueille
existed, against
.I bin/find\-open\-copy.py
\- itself written because two research passes rediscovered the same three
indexes by hand. The page states the promise; the implementation is judged
against it.
